    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hi, my tomato plants are acting weird. the leaves at the bottom are turning yellow but the ones at the top look okay. what do you think is going on and how can i fix the soil?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yellowing lower leaves on tomatoes often points to a nitrogen deficiency, since the plant moves nitrogen to the new growth at the top, sacrificing the old leaves. It can also be a sign of overwatering or a magnesium shortage. To fix the soil, start by testing the drainage—if it's soggy, let it dry out before watering again. Then, work in a balanced organic fertilizer (like compost or a 5-5-5 blend) and consider a foliar spray of diluted Epsom salt (1 tbsp per gallon) for magnesium. If the yellowing spreads upward, check for root rot or pests like aphids.",
      "source": "target",
