    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the info. i'm not sure about the nitrogen thing though. it feels like they're just... losing it. like something is actively taking the nutrients out of the plant before it can even use them? does that make sense?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That makes perfect sense, and it's a very astute observation. What you're describing sounds like the plant is being drained from the inside out—a classic sign of a systemic issue, not just a simple nutrient shortage. \n\nTwo main culprits fit that \"actively stealing\" feeling: **root-knot nematodes** (microscopic worms that hijack the roots and siphon off nutrients before the plant can use them) or a **vascular wilt** like fusarium, which clogs the water and nutrient highways. \n\nTo check for nematodes, gently dig up a bit of the root ball—if you see swollen, knobby galls, that's your thief. For fusarium, cut a lower stem lengthwise; if the inside is brown or streaked, that's the culprit. \n\nIf it's nematodes, you'll need to solarize the soil (cover with clear plastic for 4-6 weeks in full sun) and plant resistant varieties next year. For fusarium, remove the plant and avoid planting tomatoes in that spot for 3-4 years. \n\nAlso, test your soil pH—if it's too acidic (below 6.0), nutrients get locked out, which feels exactly like",
      "source": "target",
