    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're spot on—it does feel like a looting, and you're right to suspect a living culprit. The best way to tell if it's just poor soil chemistry versus an active invader is to do a **soil test** (for pH and nutrient levels) and a **root inspection** (for nematodes or rot). \n\nIf the soil test comes back balanced but the roots are gnarled or have tiny knots, that's your smoking gun—nematodes are literally mining your plant's roots. If the roots look clean but the stem is brown inside, that's a fungal thief like fusarium. \n\nFor a quick home check, dig up a small root sample and rinse it—healthy roots are white and firm, while nematode-damaged roots look like they've been chewed on by tiny witches. Also, smell the soil: a sour, rotten odor means anaerobic bacteria are hogging the nutrients. \n\nIf you want to be extra sure, send a soil sample to a local extension lab—they'll tell you if it's a nutrient lockout or a biological pest. In the meantime, try a **biochar or compost tea** drench to boost beneficial microbes that can outcompete the bad",
